Tasks Module and Service Architecture
Controllers, services, dependency injection, and a clean Prisma boundary

Phase 11 - Persistent Task CRUD API
Volume 03 - Database Engineering

Architecture milestone
HTTP routing

↓

TasksService behavior

↓

PrismaService persistence

Phase 11 replaces process memory with durable PostgreSQL-backed application behavior.

The module we actually need
The old TodosModule stored data in memory. Phase 11 introduces TasksModule because Task is now the
durable domain resource. We do not create repository abstractions yet; PrismaService already gives the
service a clean database dependency.

src/tasks/
tasks.module.ts
tasks.controller.ts
tasks.service.ts
tasks.types.ts

Module wiring
import { Module } from "@nestjs/common";
import { TasksController } from "./tasks.controller";
import { TasksService } from "./tasks.service";

@Module({
controllers: [TasksController],
providers: [TasksService],
})
export class TasksModule {}

PrismaModule is global from Phase 09, so TasksModule does not need to import it explicitly. TasksService can
request PrismaService through Nest dependency injection.

Application module
import { Module } from "@nestjs/common";
import { ConfigModule } from "@nestjs/config";
import { HealthModule } from "./health/health.module";
import { PrismaModule } from "./prisma/prisma.module";
import { TasksModule } from "./tasks/tasks.module";

@Module({
imports: [
ConfigModule.forRoot({ isGlobal: true }),
PrismaModule,
HealthModule,
TasksModule,
],
})
export class AppModule {}

Why remove TodosModule from AppModule?

The old /todos endpoint represented temporary storage. Leaving it registered beside /tasks would create two
competing resource models and confuse the learning architecture.

Compile-time request shapes - temporary

import {
TaskPriority,
TaskStatus,
} from "../generated/prisma/client";

// These are compile-time shapes only. Phase 12 replaces this temporary
// boundary with DTOs + runtime validation.
export type CreateTaskInput = {
projectId: string;
title: string;
description?: string | null;
};

export type UpdateTaskInput = {
title?: string;
description?: string | null;
status?: TaskStatus;
priority?: TaskPriority;
};

These TypeScript types help the editor and compiler, but they do not validate JSON received from the network.
A caller can still send an invalid string at runtime. Phase 12 replaces this temporary boundary with DTOs and
runtime validation.

Service dependency graph
Figure 1 - Nest dependency graph
TasksController

↓

TasksService

↓

PrismaService

↓

Prisma Client + adapter

↓

PostgreSQL

Nest wires class dependencies; Prisma handles persistence; the database remains the final integrity boundary.

Controller vs service
Controller owns                                                 Service owns

Routes and HTTP decorators                                      Task application behavior

Path/body extraction                                            Project existence checks

HTTP status choice                                              Prisma queries

Delegation                                                      Not-found semantics

NestJS guidance consistently treats controllers as request handlers that delegate more complex work to
injectable providers/services. That separation is the pattern TaskFlow follows here.

