Read Persistent Tasks
Collection and detail queries with meaningful not-found behavior

Phase 11 - Persistent Task CRUD API
Volume 03 - Database Engineering

Architecture milestone
GET /tasks

↓

Prisma findMany/findUnique

↓

200 or 404

Phase 11 replaces process memory with durable PostgreSQL-backed application behavior.

GET /tasks - list durable rows
A read operation should not know whether the row was created five seconds ago or before the API restarted.
That is exactly what durable storage gives us.

findAll() {
return this.prisma.task.findMany({
orderBy: { createdAt: "desc" },
});
}

This phase keeps list behavior deliberately small. Filtering, search, sorting policy, and pagination become a
dedicated professional API topic in Phase 15.

Controller list route
@Get()
findAll() {
return this.tasksService.findAll();
}

Test the collection
Invoke-RestMethod `
-Method Get `
-Uri "http://127.0.0.1:4000/tasks"

GET /tasks/:id - one resource
findUnique() may return null. HTTP clients should not have to understand Prisma nullability, so the service
translates absence into an HTTP-aware application error.

async findOne(id: string) {
const task = await this.prisma.task.findUnique({
where: { id },
});

if (!task) {
throw new NotFoundException(
`Task ${id} was not found.`,
);
}

return task;
}

@Get(":id")
findOne(@Param("id") id: string) {
return this.tasksService.findOne(id);
}

PowerShell detail test
$taskId = "<TASK-ID>"
Invoke-RestMethod `
-Method Get `
-Uri "http://127.0.0.1:4000/tasks/$taskId"

Missing resource semantics

Requesting a random Task id should return 404 Not Found. That is an API contract decision. Clients should not
receive a raw Prisma error or a false 200 response with null.

Figure 1 - Read decision
GET /tasks/:id

↓

