↓

row exists?

↓

yes -> 200 Task | no -> 404

The service translates storage results into application semantics.

Update Persistent Tasks
PATCH semantics, Prisma update, and P2025 translation

Phase 11 - Persistent Task CRUD API
Volume 03 - Database Engineering

Architecture milestone
PATCH /tasks/:id

↓

Prisma update

↓

durable row changes

Phase 11 replaces process memory with durable PostgreSQL-backed application behavior.

PATCH /tasks/:id - partial durable change
PATCH is appropriate when the client changes only part of a Task. The update body in this phase is
intentionally small: title, description, status, and priority.

@Patch(":id")
update(
@Param("id") id: string,
@Body() body: UpdateTaskInput,
) {
return this.tasksService.update(id, body);
}

Prisma update
async update(id: string, input: UpdateTaskInput) {
try {
return await this.prisma.task.update({
where: { id },
data: input,
});
} catch (error) {
this.rethrowMissingTask(error, id);
}
}

Prisma performs a targeted UPDATE for the row selected by the unique id. updatedAt changes automatically
because the schema marks it with @updatedAt.

Why catch P2025?
For the locked Prisma 7 client used by this course, an update or delete that depends on a missing record can
raise PrismaClientKnownRequestError with code P2025. We translate that database-client error into 404 for
the HTTP contract.

private rethrowMissingTask(
error: unknown,
id: string,
): never {
if (
error instanceof Prisma.PrismaClientKnownRequestError &&
error.code === "P2025"
) {
throw new NotFoundException(
`Task ${id} was not found.`,
);
}

throw error;
}

PowerShell update

$patch = @{
status = "DONE"
priority = "HIGH"
} | ConvertTo-Json

Invoke-RestMethod `
-Method Patch `
-Uri "http://127.0.0.1:4000/tasks/$taskId" `
-ContentType "application/json" `
-Body $patch

Important limitation

TypeScript says status is TaskStatus, but raw JSON is still untrusted at runtime. Phase 12 will reject values such as
NOT_A_REAL_STATUS before they reach Prisma.

Business state comes later
Phase 11 permits direct status changes because the goal is database CRUD. Larger workflows may eventually
require rules such as DONE -> CANCELED being forbidden, or completedAt being synchronized. Those are
service/business-rule concerns, not reasons to overload this persistence lesson.

End-to-End Persistent TaskFlow
Reconnect the thin Next.js view and debug across layers

Phase 11 - Persistent Task CRUD API
Volume 03 - Database Engineering

Architecture milestone
Next.js Server Component

↓

NestJS /tasks

↓

PostgreSQL-backed HTML

Phase 11 replaces process memory with durable PostgreSQL-backed application behavior.

Reconnect the thin Next.js view
The backend is the focus of Phase 11, but a short integration check keeps the full-stack mental model alive.
Next.js can now read the durable /tasks resource instead of the old in-memory /todos endpoint.

export type Task = {
id: string;
projectId: string;
title: string;
description: string | null;
status: "TODO" | "IN_PROGRESS" | "DONE" | "CANCELED";
priority: "LOW" | "MEDIUM" | "HIGH" | "URGENT";
createdAt: string;
updatedAt: string;
};

const API_BASE_URL = process.env.API_BASE_URL;

if (!API_BASE_URL) {
throw new Error("API_BASE_URL is required.");
}

export async function getTasks(): Promise<Task[]> {
const response = await fetch(`${API_BASE_URL}/tasks`, {
cache: "no-store",
});

if (!response.ok) {
throw new Error(`Task API returned ${response.status}.`);
}

return response.json();
}

The API base URL remains server-only in this example because the Server Component performs the request.
No database URL or database credential is ever exposed to the browser.

Task page
import { getTasks } from "@/lib/api/tasks";

export default async function TasksPage() {
const tasks = await getTasks();

return (
<main>
<h1>Tasks</h1>

{tasks.length === 0 ? (
<p>No persisted tasks yet.</p>
) : (
<ul>
{tasks.map((task) => (
<li key={task.id}>
<strong>{task.title}</strong>
{" - "}
{task.status}
</li>
))}
</ul>
)}
</main>
);
}

Figure 1 - End-to-end durable read

Browser requests /tasks page

↓

Next.js Server Component

↓

GET NestJS /tasks

↓

TasksService

↓

PrismaService

↓

PostgreSQL

↓

HTML returned to browser

The browser still talks to an application layer. It never connects directly to PostgreSQL.

End-to-end test
• Create a Task with PowerShell against NestJS.

• Refresh the Next.js /tasks page.

• Confirm the new Task title and status render.

• Restart NestJS, refresh Next.js again, and confirm the Task still renders.

Debug by layer
Observation                                                  Likely layer to inspect first

NestJS /tasks fails in PowerShell                            Backend / Prisma / database

PowerShell succeeds, Next.js fails                           Next.js API client / environment URL

API returns [] but SQL shows rows                            Prisma query / wrong database connection

SQL has no row after POST                                    Create path / migration / DB connection

Rows disappear only after restart                            You are still hitting memory somewhere

Still intentionally simple

No mutation UI, optimistic update, TanStack Query cache, authentication, or authorization is added yet. Those later
phases should solve real problems one at a time.

